1. Project purpose

Working name: Church Records and Ministry Management System
Initial market: Ghanaian churches
Primary users: Church secretaries, treasurers, pastors, ministry leaders, and designated administrative officers.

Objective: Provide a coordinated system for recording church activities, managing membership and resources, and compiling accurate reports with less administrative effort.

Begin with one church in the pilot. Serving multiple independent churches will require a separate design for church registration, subscriptions, and strict separation of each church’s records.

2. System structure

The completed application will contain these sections, introduced according to our phases:
Section	Purpose
Dashboard	Summarise activities, results, and outstanding work
Calendar and Programmes	Schedule activities and connect them to service records
Attendance	Record service attendance and trends
Membership	Maintain member profiles, roles, groups, and status history
Visitors and Follow-up	Track visits, assigned officers, and follow-up progress
Evangelism and Discipleship	Record souls won and subsequent discipleship
Finance	Maintain income, expenditure, funds, and reconciliation
Inventory	Track church assets and their condition
Reports and Documents	Compile, review, approve, and retrieve reports and files
Settings and Users	Manage church details, categories, and permissions


3. Calendar and daily activity records

The calendar will offer daily, weekly, and monthly views, with recurring programmes and special events.

Each programme will have:
- Programme title and unique reference.
- Date, start/end time, and venue.
- Programme type and responsible ministry.
- Assigned officer.
- Status: scheduled, completed, postponed, or cancelled.
- Linked attendance, offerings, visitors, souls won, and activity notes.
A programme can be completed while its report remains awaiting submission or review. These are separate statuses.

4. Attendance rules

Preserve this entry order:
Category	Entry fields
Pastor	Number present
Elders	Number present
Deacons	Number present
Deaconesses	Number present
Members	Male and female
Children	Male and female
Total	Automatically calculated


For the pilot:
- Count each person once per service.
- Officers must not also appear under ordinary members.
- Use zero for a completed category with nobody present; a blank means the count is incomplete.
- Record visitors as a separate breakdown within total attendance, not an additional amount added to it.
- The adult member columns will initially mean adult congregants excluding officers, including adult visitors. Display this explanation beside the form; we can adjust the labels to match the pilot church’s reporting convention.
- Aggregate attendance measures visits. Unique attendees require individual check-in records.
5. Membership, visitors, and souls won
Record	Minimum information
Member	Unique ID, name, sex, contact details where available, church role, ministry/group, membership date, and status
Visitor	Name where provided, visit date, linked programme, first-time/returning status, optional contact details, and assigned follow-up officer
Evangelistic response	Person or contact reference, response date, programme/source, assigned officer, and follow-up status
Follow-up	Responsible officer, next action, due date, outcome, and restricted notes where necessary


Use “Souls won” as the church-facing label, defined for reporting as a newly recorded profession of faith. Keep baptism, discipleship, and membership admission as separate milestones.

Link repeat visits and follow-ups to an existing person where identifiable. Do not count the same person repeatedly as newly won.

For the pilot, membership statuses will be Active, Inactive, Transferred Out, and Deceased, with effective dates and retained history. Final definitions should match the church’s practice.

6. Financial records

Currency: GHS
Payment methods: Cash, bank, and mobile money.
Income categories	Expenditure categories
Free will	Utilities
Offering	Support
Special offering	Welfare
Other income	Purchases
	Repairs and maintenance
	Transport and travel
	Programmes and events
	Administration
	Other expenditure


Every transaction should have a date, unique reference, amount, category, account, fund, description, recorder, and supporting evidence reference.

Financial controls will include:
- Separate transaction entry and review.
- No self-approval where approval is required.
- Account and fund opening balances.
- Transfers recorded separately from income and expenditure.
- Reconciliation against actual cash, bank, and MoMo balances.
- Corrections to approved entries through traceable reversals or adjustments.
- Special offerings linked to their designated purposes.
This starts as a cash receipts, payments, and fund-tracking system. Full accrual accounting is outside the initial scope.

7. Dashboard and reporting

The dashboard will offer daily, weekly, monthly, quarterly, half-year, annual, and custom-date summaries, subject to each user’s permissions.
Measure	Reporting treatment
Programmes	Count by scheduled date and status
Attendance visits	Sum completed attendance records
Average attendance	Total attendance divided by services with completed counts
First-time visitors	Count identifiable first-time visitors once
Souls won	Count newly recorded professions of faith without duplicates
Income and expenditure	Sum posted transactions within the period
Membership	Show status as at the reporting end date
Inventory	Show asset position as at a stated date once history is supported


Default reporting conventions:
- Week: Monday–Sunday.
- Quarters: January–March, April–June, July–September, October–December.
- Half-years: January–June and July–December.
- Year: January–December.
- Date display: DD/MM/YYYY.
- Time zone: Africa/Accra.
Keep these configurable if the church uses a different reporting year.

Reports will show the church, period, preparation date, preparer, reviewer, approval status, and explanatory notes. Approved reports will retain dated versions.

8. User access
Role	Default access
Administrator	Manage users, settings, and access; sensitive record access granted explicitly
Secretary	Programmes, attendance, membership, and administrative reports
Treasurer	Financial entry, reconciliation, and financial reports
Ministry leader	Records for assigned ministries
Follow-up officer	Assigned visitor and discipleship records
Leadership reviewer	Authorised reports and designated approvals
Inventory custodian	Asset records and verification


Every user receives an individual account. Access must protect underlying records and exports, as well as visible pages. Role changes and important record changes must be logged.

9. First Base44 build

Our first build will implement:
1. Sign-in and the initial user permissions.
2. Church settings.
3. Calendar and programme management.
4. Aggregate attendance entry.
5. Basic visitor and souls-won counts linked to programmes.
6. A daily, weekly, and monthly activity dashboard.
7. Fictional demonstration records.
Detailed person-based follow-up follows in Phase 3. Finance, inventory, and advanced reports will appear as later-phase features until implemented.

10. Acceptance criteria

The first build is ready for review when:
- An authorised officer can create and edit a programme.
- Recurring activities appear correctly in the calendar.
- A complete attendance record calculates correctly.
- Blank counts are flagged rather than treated as zero.
- Visitor figures do not inflate total attendance.
- Date filters produce correct activity summaries.
- Records remain saved after signing out and returning.
- Users cannot view or change records outside their permissions.
- Duplicate programme attendance submissions are prevented or clearly flagged.
